@startuml isbm-roundtrip
' ws-CIR BODs over ws-ISBM — the flow exercised by Testing/test-isbm-roundtrip.ps1
' Render: java -jar plantuml.jar docs/isbm-roundtrip-sequence.puml

title ws-CIR over ws-ISBM — end-to-end round trip

skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam maxMessageSize 260
skinparam BoxPadding 12

actor "test-isbm-roundtrip.ps1\n(consumer + publisher)" as Test

box "ws-ISBM Provider" #F4F7FB
    participant "REST API" as ISBM
    participant "Durable Entity\n(session state)" as Entity
end box

queue "Service Bus\nreq-/resp-/pub-<hash>" as SB

box "ws-CIR Provider" #F7F4FB
    participant "IsbmBodListener" as Listener
    participant "BodDispatcher" as Dispatcher
    participant "SqlCirStore" as Store
end box

database "Azure SQL" as SQL

autonumber

== 01  Provider is listening ==

Test -> Listener : GET /api/isbm/status
Listener -> SQL : SELECT cir.IsbmSession
SQL --> Listener : ProviderRequest + Subscription session ids
Listener --> Test : enabled, topics, open sessions

note over Listener, SQL
  Session ids are persisted. On Consumption the host recycles
  freely, and re-opening per poll would leak sessions on the
  broker that still accumulate messages nobody reads.
end note

== 02  Request-response: ProcessRegistry -> AcknowledgeRegistry ==

Test -> ISBM : POST /consumer-request-sessions\n{ channelUri, topics, listenerUrl, ... }
ISBM -> Entity : signal open (asynchronous)
ISBM --> Test : { sessionId }

note right of Test #FFF3CD
  **Race.** The id is returned before the entity commits.
  Using the session immediately yields
  "Session does not exist or wrong type" — the same
  message as a genuine type mismatch.
  The script waits and retries on {"fault":"Session"}.
end note

Test -> ISBM : POST /sessions/{consumerId}/requests\n{ mediaType, **inlineContent** = ProcessRegistry BOD }
activate ISBM
ISBM -> SB : send -> req-<hash> queue
ISBM --> Test : { messageId }
deactivate ISBM

note right of Test #FFF3CD
  **inlineContent**, not content. It is optional, so a wrong
  name returns 200 with a messageId and silently discards
  the payload. mediaType is required and fails loudly.
end note

Test -> Listener : POST /api/isbm/drain

activate Listener
Listener -> SQL : read ProviderRequest session id
Listener -> ISBM : GET /sessions/{providerId}/request
ISBM -> SB : receive from req-<hash>
SB --> ISBM : message
ISBM --> Listener : { messageId, messageContent.inlineContent }

Listener -> Dispatcher : DispatchAsync(XDocument)
activate Dispatcher
Dispatcher -> Dispatcher : parse envelope\nverb = oa:Process, noun = cir:CreateRegistry\nacknowledgeCode = Always
Dispatcher -> Store : CreateRegistryAsync(...)
Store -> SQL : INSERT Registry / Category / Entry\n(single ReadCommitted transaction)
SQL --> Store : committed
Store --> Dispatcher : ok
Dispatcher -> Dispatcher : build AcknowledgeRegistry\nno fault elements = success
Dispatcher --> Listener : AcknowledgeRegistry BOD
deactivate Dispatcher

Listener -> ISBM : POST /sessions/{providerId}/response\n{ requestMessageId, messageContent }
ISBM -> SB : send -> resp-<hash> topic
Listener -> ISBM : DELETE /sessions/{providerId}/request
note left of Listener
  Removal is unconditional. Leaving the head in place
  replays it forever; an unreadable message is removed
  and reported in the drain report's `discarded` array.
end note
Listener --> Test : { requestsHandled: 1, responsesPosted: 1, errors: [] }
deactivate Listener

Test -> Store : POST /api/queries/registry (verify the entry landed)
Store -> SQL : SELECT with REGEXP_LIKE predicate
SQL --> Store : Registry graph
Store --> Test : entry with CIRID intact

Test -> ISBM : GET /sessions/{consumerId}/requests/{requestMessageId}/response
ISBM -> SB : receive from resp-<hash> subscription
SB --> ISBM : message
ISBM --> Test : AcknowledgeRegistry

note right of Test
  OriginalApplicationArea sits **inside** oa:Acknowledge
  (ResponseVerbType), echoing the original BODID.
  Annex A also permits omitting it when other correlation
  exists — over ISBM that is OriginalMessageID.
end note

Test -> ISBM : DELETE /sessions/{consumerId}/requests/{requestMessageId}/response

== 03  Publication: CancelRegistry has no response ==

Test -> ISBM : POST /publication-sessions\n{ channelUri = /OIIE/CIR/Publication }
ISBM --> Test : { sessionId }

Test -> ISBM : POST /sessions/{publisherId}/publications\n{ mediaType, inlineContent = CancelRegistry BOD }
ISBM -> SB : publish -> pub-<hash> topic
ISBM --> Test : { messageId }

Test -> Listener : POST /api/isbm/drain
activate Listener
Listener -> ISBM : GET /sessions/{subscriptionId}/publication
ISBM -> SB : receive from subscription
SB --> ISBM : message
ISBM --> Listener : CancelRegistry BOD

Listener -> Dispatcher : DispatchAsync(XDocument)
activate Dispatcher
Dispatcher -> Dispatcher : verb = oa:Cancel, noun = cir:DeleteRegistry
Dispatcher -> Store : DeleteRegistryAsync("ISBM-Roundtrip")
Store -> SQL : DELETE cir.Registry\n(ON DELETE CASCADE removes Categories, Entries, Properties)
SQL --> Store : committed
Dispatcher --> Listener : **null** — the catalogue defines no response BOD
deactivate Dispatcher

Listener -> ISBM : DELETE /sessions/{subscriptionId}/publication
Listener --> Test : { publicationsHandled: 1, responsesPosted: 0 }
deactivate Listener

note over Listener, Dispatcher #D4EDDA
  The four Cancel BODs and ChangeEntryCIRID define no
  response, which is why they travel as **publications**
  rather than requests. A publication that produces a
  response BOD arrived on the wrong channel and is logged
  as a warning.
end note

Test -> Store : POST /api/queries/registry (verify it is gone)
Store --> Test : { registry: [] }

== Teardown ==

Test -> ISBM : DELETE /consumer-request-sessions/{consumerId}
Test -> ISBM : DELETE /publication-sessions/{publisherId}

note over Test, ISBM
  Closing is per session type. There is no shared
  DELETE /sessions/{id}, even though every other
  session-scoped route is /sessions/{id}/...
end note

@enduml
